 exit. It opens no store, port or platform connection until it owns the
 profile. While it waits, `gateway.standby` exists in `HERMES_HOME`, so a
 supervisor can stop the old gateway only once its successor is ready (for
-example a Kubernetes `preStop` hook that waits for the file). When the wait runs out it exits as before. Set the wait above the old
+example a Kubernetes `preStop` hook that waits for the file). The waiting gateway
+touches the file every 2 seconds and writes its host name in it (`hostname`), so a
+file older than 10 seconds is left over from a gateway that was killed while it
+waited: readers ignore it, and the next gateway that owns the profile removes it. A
+probe that skips its health check for a standby should test the same, for example
+`find "$HERMES_HOME/gateway.standby" -newermt '-10 seconds' | grep -q . && grep -qF "\"hostname\": \"$(cat /etc/hostname)\"" "$HERMES_HOME/gateway.standby"`.
+When the wait runs out it exits as before. Set the wait above the old
 gateway's drain (`agent.restart_drain_timeout`) plus its shutdown. The default
 `0` keeps the immediate exit.
 
